  <Contracts>
    <Contract id="ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_FLOW">
      SDD v2 has exactly two mandatory semantic approval boundaries.

      1. **Spec approval.** The complete intended spec set exists as actual files, passes structural
         checks, and receives one fresh independent semantic review of the current artifacts. The author
         resolves findings. Any semantic edit invalidates the old review and requires one fresh review
         of the changed set. The operator then approves the specs themselves and the canonical specs
         record `ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER` approval #1.
      2. **Ticket approval.** Actual tickets and indexes exist, each ticket passes the mechanical
         authoring check, and one fresh independent reviewer checks the complete actual ticket set
         against the approved specs. The operator then approves task decomposition and the test plan in
         one decision. The owning task index records `ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER` approval #2. Any semantic
         ticket or index edit invalidates the old review and approval.

      Reviewers are bounded calls, not durable sessions. They never edit targets and do not exchange
      JSON state with the orchestrator. The author may reason about its own draft before dispatch, but
      self-review never substitutes for the independent review at either boundary.

      Resume state is always reconstructible from human-readable approval markers, specs, tickets,
      Execution Log, Git, and current tool output. Mechanical checks validate marker shape and membership;
      the model uses Git evidence to judge semantic staleness. No `.sdd-session.md`, plan digest, critic
      journal, content hash, or worker checkpoint is part of correctness.
    </Contract>
    <Contract id="ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER">
      Approval evidence is ordinary, human-readable content in the canonical artifact. It is not a
      session record, JSON sidecar, digest, fingerprint, or byte-level lock.

      Approval #1 is a Decision Log entry in every specification in the reviewed set:

      ```markdown
      ### Approval #1 — current specification set
      - **Status:** pending | approved
      - **Reviewed set:** `specs/...`, `specs/...`
      - **Independent review:** pending | clean | findings resolved
      - **Operator decision:** pending | approved
      - **Recorded:** YYYY-MM-DD
      ```

      Approval #2 is a section in the narrowest task index that owns the complete reviewed ticket set
      (module, scope, or project index):

      ```markdown
      ## Approval #2 — decomposition and test plan
      - **Status:** pending | approved
      - **Reviewed tickets:** `specs/...task....md`, `specs/...task....md`
      - **Independent review:** pending | clean | findings resolved
      - **Operator decision:** pending | approved
      - **Recorded:** YYYY-MM-DD
      ```

      Authoring starts with `pending`. A clean independent review updates only the review field. Operator
      approval changes `Status` and `Operator decision` to `approved`. These metadata-only marker edits do
      not require another review. Any later semantic edit to a listed spec, ticket, or owning index resets
      the applicable marker to `pending`; for approval #1, reset every marker whose Reviewed set contains
      the edited path. If staleness is uncertain, treat the marker as pending and repeat the boundary.

      Mechanical checks own only marker presence, accepted field values, path existence, and membership.
      The model owns the semantic question whether current Git changes make an apparently approved marker
      stale. Git diff/history are evidence for that judgment, not a cryptographic gate. The agent never
      computes or copies a content hash for approval.
    </Contract>
    <Contract id="TASK_TICKET_STRUCTURE">
      Sections are wrapped with `<!--SECTION:UPPER_SNAKE-->…<!--/SECTION:UPPER_SNAKE-->` markers
      (grammar: the `sdd-extract` CLI, name grammar `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*$`). Anchor names are normative per
      `AX_TICKET_SECTION_NAMES_NORMATIVE` — orchestrator (`sdd-execute`) and auditor extract by exact
      string (`extract <ticket> PHASE_P<N>`, `META`, `EXECUTION_LOG`, …). Each `### P<N>` phase block
      under `Phases` gets its own anchor (`PHASE_P1`, `PHASE_P2`, …); the `Phases` container
      itself carries NO anchor — extraction is per phase.

      Spec content is referenced, never copied (`AX_SSOT_TRACEABILITY`): tickets link to spec sections
      via Markdown anchors — module spec for contracts / consumers / invariants; scope spec Requirements & Constraints only for
      cross-cutting constraints. Shared per-module content
      (Execution-Log token vocabulary, baseline Completion Rule, audit hint, file-header rule) lives
      once in `<module>.3-tasks.md` per `AX_TICKET_DEDUPLICATION` — not duplicated here.

      Approval #2 is not copied into every ticket. The narrowest owning task index lists the complete
      reviewed ticket set and carries the human-readable `ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER`; execution resolves
      that index and requires the selected ticket to be a listed member.

